.\" The man page of glaze. cmd/glaze/man_test.go checks that it names every
.\" command, every flag, the profile name and every environment variable.
.TH GLAZE 1 "2026-11-07" "Glazier" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
glaze \- manage tmux sessions, windows and panes from a profile
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B glaze
.RI [ global\ options ]
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B glaze up
.RB [ \-\-detached ]
.RB [ \-\-clear ]
.RB [ \-\-profile\-path
.IR path ]
.RB [ \-\-var
.IR key=value ]
.RI [ name ]
.br
.B glaze down
.RB [ \-\-session
.IR session ]
.RI [ name ]
.br
.B glaze ls
.RB [ \-\-profiles ]
.br
.B glaze format
.RB [ \-\-validate ]
.RB [ \-\-stdout ]
.RI [ name ]
.br
.B glaze save
.RB [ \-\-session
.IR session ]
.RB [ \-\-stdout ]
.RI [ name ]
.br
.B glaze completion
.IR shell
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B glaze
creates a tmux session with its windows and panes from a profile. A profile is a
.B .glaze
file in the HCL language. It declares the session, each window, each pane, the
commands that run in a pane and the tmux options and hooks.
.B glaze
validates the profile before it starts tmux, so an error in the profile stops
the command before a session exists.
.PP
A profile name after
.BR up ,
.B down
or
.B format
selects a profile in the profile registry, from any directory. A profile name
after
.B save
writes the profile
.IB name .glaze
into the profile registry. See
.BR PROFILES .
.PP
A profile runs its commands and hooks in your shell, with your permissions.
Read a profile from another person before you apply it.
.B glaze format \-\-validate
checks the syntax, not what the commands do.
.PP
.B glaze
needs tmux 3.2a or later on
.BR PATH .
.B up
and
.B save
check the version first and stop with exit status 4 on an older tmux.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS up
Apply a profile. The command creates the session, the windows and the panes,
runs the commands of each pane and attaches your terminal to the session. When
a session with the same name runs, the command attaches to it and changes
nothing.
.PP
A profile name selects the profile
.IB name .glaze
in the profile registry. The session name comes from the profile, not from the
profile name. A profile in the profile registry must set the session name, or
the command stops with exit status 3. Put the flags before or after the name.
Do not give a profile name together with
.BR \-\-profile\-path .
.TP
.B \-\-detached
Create the session and do not attach to it.
.TP
.B \-\-clear
First kill an existing session that has the same name.
.B glaze
refuses when it runs inside that session, because the kill would also end
.BR glaze .
.TP
.B \-\-keep\-on\-failure
Keep the partly built session when
.B up
fails, so that you can examine it. Without the flag,
.B glaze
removes the session that this run created.
.TP
.B \-\-debug
Print each command that
.B glaze
sends to the tmux socket, and the text of each pane and session command. Env
values show as
.BR <redacted> .
.TP
.BI \-\-command\-timeout " duration"
Stop the wait for the commands of a pane after this duration, for example
.BR 5m .
The default value
.B 0
waits with no limit.
.TP
.BI \-\-profile\-path " path"
The path to a
.B .glaze
file. See
.BR PROFILES .
Not together with a profile name.
.TP
.BI \-\-socket\-path " path"
The path to a custom tmux socket.
.TP
.BI \-\-socket\-name " name"
The name of a custom tmux socket.
.TP
.BI \-\-var " key=value"
Set a variable. The flag is repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-\-var\-file " path"
An HCL file of variable values. The path is relative to the current directory,
also with a profile name.
.SS down
Kill the session that a profile describes. The command evaluates only the
session name of the profile. A session that does not run causes no error.
.PP
A profile name selects the same profile as for
.BR up ,
and the command reads the session name from that profile. Do not give a
profile name together with
.BR \-\-session .
When the profile is gone from the profile registry, kill the session with
.BR \-\-session .
.TP
.BI \-\-session " session"
Kill the session with this name. No profile is required.
.TP
.BI \-\-profile\-path " path"
.TQ
.BI \-\-socket\-path " path"
.TQ
.BI \-\-socket\-name " name"
.TQ
.BI \-\-var " key=value"
.TQ
.BI \-\-var\-file " path"
As for
.BR up .
.SS ls
List the sessions that run on the tmux server: the name, the number of
windows, the path and a marker for the session of the pane that runs the
command. The command succeeds with an empty table when no server runs.
.TP
.B \-\-profiles
List the profiles in the profile registry instead of the sessions: the name
and the path of each one. The command reads only the profile registry and
needs no tmux. With no profile registry, the command writes nothing to
standard output, logs
.B "no profile registry"
and exits with status 0. It warns about an entry that
.B up
cannot use.
.TP
.BI \-\-socket\-path " path"
.TQ
.BI \-\-socket\-name " name"
As for
.BR up .
.SS format
Rewrite a profile in the canonical format. The command writes the file through
a temporary file and one rename, and leaves a file that is already formatted
as it is.
.PP
A profile name selects the profile in the profile registry and formats it in
place. With
.B \-\-stdout
and
.BR \-\-validate ,
a profile name works as a path does.
.B glaze format \-\-validate
with a profile name checks a new profile in the profile registry without
tmux.
.TP
.B \-\-stdout
Print the formatted profile and do not write the file.
.TP
.B \-\-validate
Check the profile and print each diagnostic. Every declared variable must get
a value from
.B \-\-var
or from a default.
.TP
.BI \-\-profile\-path " path"
.TQ
.BI \-\-var " key=value"
.TQ
.BI \-\-var\-file " path"
As for
.BR up .
.SS save
Write the structure of a running session as a profile: the names of the
session, the windows and the panes, their directories, the active window and
pane, and the layout of each window. The command does not save commands,
environment variables, hooks or options, because tmux reports the whole state
and not what a profile set, and a saved command would run again on each
.BR up .
A profile name writes the profile
.IB name .glaze
into the profile registry. When the registry is missing, the command creates
it with mode 0700, only after a successful capture and right before the
write, so a failed capture creates nothing. With
.BR \-\-stdout ,
the command prints the profile and writes nothing. A profile name combines
with
.BR \-\-session ,
which selects the session to save. Do not give a profile name together with
.BR \-\-profile\-path .
.TP
.BI \-\-profile\-path " path"
The path of the profile to write. The default is
.B .glaze
in the current directory. The command refuses an existing file. Not together
with a profile name.
.TP
.BI \-\-session " session"
Save the session with this name. The default is the session that
.B glaze
runs in.
.TP
.B \-\-stdout
Print the profile and do not write a file.
.TP
.B \-\-force
Replace the target profile when it exists.
.TP
.BI \-\-socket\-path " path"
.TQ
.BI \-\-socket\-name " name"
As for
.BR up .
.SS completion
Print a completion script for
.BR bash ,
.BR zsh ,
.B fish
or
.BR pwsh .
The script asks
.B glaze
for the commands and the flags on each Tab. After
.BR up ,
.B down
and
.BR format ,
Tab also lists the names of the profile registry. The packages install the
scripts. With a release zip or
.BR "go install" ,
load the script yourself:
.PP
.RS
.nf
eval "$(glaze completion bash)"        # add to ~/.bashrc
source <(glaze completion zsh)         # add to ~/.zshrc, after compinit
mkdir -p ~/.config/fish/completions && glaze completion fish > ~/.config/fish/completions/glaze.fish
.fi
.RE
.PP
Use
.B eval
for bash. The form
.B "source <(...)"
does nothing in bash 3.2, which is the bash that macOS includes.
.SS help
Show the help of
.B glaze
or of one command. Write
.B glaze help up
or
.BR "glaze up \-\-help" .
The word
.B help
after a command is a profile name.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
Put a global option before the command.
.TP
.BI \-\-log\-level " level"
One of
.BR trace ,
.BR debug ,
.BR info ,
.BR warning ,
.B error
or
.BR critical .
The default is
.BR info .
.TP
.BR \-\-version ", " \-v
Print the version, the stage, the commit and the build date.
.B glaze up \-v
is a usage error.
.TP
.BR \-\-help ", " \-h
Show the help.
.SH PROFILES
A profile name after
.BR up ,
.B down
or
.B format
selects the profile
.IB name .glaze
in the profile registry and nothing else: not the current directory and not
.BR GLAZE_PATH .
A profile name after
.B save
writes that file. Without a profile name,
.B glaze
looks for a profile in this order: the
.B \-\-profile\-path
value,
.B .glaze
in the current directory, then
.B .glaze
in the directory that
.B GLAZE_PATH
names. A
.B ~
or
.B ~/
at the start of a path expands to your home directory.
.PP
The profile registry is one directory:
.B GLAZE_REGISTRY
when it is set, else
.B glaze
in the directory that an absolute
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
names, else
.BR ~/.config/glaze .
The rule is the same on Linux and on macOS.
Only
.B save
with a profile name creates the directory, with mode 0700. The other commands
never create it.
.B glaze ls \-\-profiles
lists the profile registry.
.PP
A profile name has only ASCII letters, digits, full stops, underscores and
hyphens. It does not start with a full stop, a hyphen or a tilde. It has no
slash and no
.B .glaze
suffix, and it has at most 128 characters. Any other word is a usage error,
and the message gives the fix. For a file outside the profile registry, use
.BR \-\-profile\-path .
Glazier does not fold case. On a case-insensitive macOS volume,
.B glaze up Work
opens work.glaze, and
.B glaze save Work \-\-force
replaces the content of work.glaze and keeps the file name. On Linux the two
names are two files.
.PP
The profile name selects the file. The session name comes from the
.B name
attribute of the profile. A profile in the profile registry must set it:
.BR up ,
.B down
and
.B format \-\-validate
stop with exit status 3 when it is missing. One profile can serve many
projects, because
.B path.base
is the name of the current directory:
.PP
.RS
.nf
session {
  name = "reg\-${path.base}"
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
A relative
.B starting_directory
is relative to the directory of the profile. For a profile in the profile
registry, that directory is the profile registry itself. Write an absolute
path, a path that starts with
.B ~
or no
.B starting_directory
at all. Without one, the session starts in the current directory.
.PP
A minimal profile:
.PP
.RS
.nf
session {
  name              = "api"
  starting_directory = "~/src/api"

  window {
    name   = "editor"
    layout = "main\-vertical"

    pane {
      name     = "edit"
      commands = ["nvim ."]
    }

    pane {
      name     = "shell"
    }
  }
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
The README of Glazier describes the full format: variables, locals, string
functions, sizes, adjustments, options, hooks and the session environment.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B GLAZE_REGISTRY
The profile registry. It replaces the XDG rule. The value must be an absolute
path, or a path that starts with
.BR ~ .
A relative value is an error.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
The directory that holds the profile registry
.BR glaze .
The default is
.BR ~/.config .
A relative value is ignored, with a warning.
.TP
.B GLAZE_PATH
The directory that holds the
.B .glaze
file that
.B glaze
uses when the current directory has none. It is not the profile registry.
.TP
.BI GLAZE_ENV_ NAME
The value of
.BI env. NAME
in a profile.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
When set,
.B glaze
writes no colour.
.B glaze
writes colour only when standard error is a terminal.
.TP
.B TMUX
Set by tmux in a pane.
.B glaze
uses it to find the server and the session that it runs in, for
.B save
and for the attach of
.BR up .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.B ls
with no server,
.B ls \-\-profiles
with no profile registry and
.B down
for a session that does not run also succeed.
.TP
.B 1
A run failed, for example because tmux rejected an option value or
.B glaze
could not write a profile.
.TP
.B 2
The command line is not correct, for example an unknown flag, a
.B \-\-var
without
.B =
or a profile name that is not valid.
.TP
.B 3
The profile has errors,
.B glaze
cannot find the profile, the profile registry has no profile with that name or
the profile registry cannot be used.
.TP
.B 4
.B glaze
cannot use tmux, for example because tmux is not on
.BR PATH ,
is older than 3.2a or the socket does not give access.
.TP
.B 130
SIGINT stopped
.BR glaze .
.TP
.B 143
SIGTERM stopped
.BR glaze .
.PP
When
.B up
fails or a signal stops it,
.B glaze
removes the session that this run created.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B .glaze
The profile in the current directory.
.TP
.BI $GLAZE_PATH /.glaze
The profile that
.B glaze
uses when the current directory has none.
.TP
.BI $GLAZE_REGISTRY/ NAME .glaze
.TQ
.BI $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/glaze/ NAME .glaze
.TQ
.BI ~/.config/glaze/ NAME .glaze
The profile
.I NAME
in the profile registry.
.B GLAZE_REGISTRY
decides the directory when it is set, else an absolute
.BR XDG_CONFIG_HOME ,
else the home directory.
.SH EXAMPLES
Apply the profile in the current directory and attach:
.PP
.RS
.nf
glaze up
.fi
.RE
.PP
Build a session in the background with a variable:
.PP
.RS
.nf
glaze up \-\-detached \-\-var district=watson
.fi
.RE
.PP
Apply the profile
.B work.glaze
from the profile registry, from any directory:
.PP
.RS
.nf
glaze up work
.fi
.RE
.PP
List the profiles in the profile registry:
.PP
.RS
.nf
glaze ls \-\-profiles
.fi
.RE
.PP
Check a profile without tmux:
.PP
.RS
.nf
glaze format \-\-validate \-\-profile\-path gig.glaze
.fi
.RE
.PP
Check a profile in the profile registry without tmux:
.PP
.RS
.nf
glaze format \-\-validate work
.fi
.RE
.PP
Save the session that you are in and print it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
glaze save \-\-stdout > saved.glaze
.fi
.RE
.PP
Save the session that you are in into the profile registry as
.BR work :
.PP
.RS
.nf
glaze save work
.fi
.RE
.PP
Kill the session of a profile in the profile registry:
.PP
.RS
.nf
glaze down work
.fi
.RE
.PP
Kill a session by name:
.PP
.RS
.nf
glaze down \-\-session api
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tmux (1)
.PP
